{
  "dialogues": [
    {
      "speaker": "O-lan",
      "speak_to": ["Wang Lung"],
      "text": "O-lan told Wang Lung that there was another thing she had to attend to before she could die."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Wang Lung",
      "speak_to": ["O-lan"],
      "text": "Wang Lung insisted that O-lan should not speak of dying and that it would please him if she did not."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "O-lan",
      "speak_to": ["Wang Lung"],
      "text": "O-lan replied that she had to die, for she felt it in her vitals waiting, but she would not die before her eldest son came home and before he wedded this good maid who was her daughter-in-law, and well she served O-lan, holding the hot water basin steadily and knowing when to bathe O-lan's face when she sweated in pain. Now O-lan wanted her son to come home, because she had to die, and she wanted him to wed this maid first, so that she might die easily, knowing that her grandson was stirred into life and a great-grandson for the old one."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Wang Lung",
      "speak_to": ["O-lan"],
      "text": "Wang Lung told O-lan that she looked twice as well as they had said and years away from death."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "O-lan",
      "speak_to": ["Wang Lung"],
      "text": "O-lan declared that she would see Wang Lung wedded and then she must die."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "O-lan",
      "speak_to": ["Wang Lung"],
      "text": "O-lan instructed Wang Lung to sit there and drink the wine and eat the rice of his marriage, for she would see it all and this would be his bed of marriage since she was soon to be finished with it and carried away."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "O-lan",
      "speak_to": ["Wang Lung"],
      "text": "O-lan asked if everyone had wine, if the sweet rice dish in the middle of the feast was very hot, if they had put full measure of lard and sugar into it, and if the eight fruits were present."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "O-lan",
      "speak_to": ["Wang Lung"],
      "text": "O-lan told Wang Lung that she was now content and that this thing in her may do as it would. She instructed her son to look to his father and his grandfather, and her daughter to look to her husband and his father and his grandfather and the poor fool in the court, there she was. And she told Wang Lung that he had no duty to any other."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "O-lan",
      "speak_to": ["Wang Lung"],
      "text": "O-lan said that, even if she was ugly, she had borne a son; although she was but a slave, there was a son in her house."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "O-lan",
      "speak_to": ["Wang Lung"],
      "text": "O-lan insisted that no one else could feed Wang Lung's son and care for him as she did. She told Wang Lung that beauty would not bear a man sons."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "dialogues": [
    {
      "speaker": "Mr. Darcy",
      "speak_to": ["Elizabeth"],
      "text": "In vain have I struggled. It will not do. My feelings will not be repressed. You must allow me to tell you how ardently I admire and love you."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Elizabeth",
      "speak_to": ["Mr. Darcy"],
      "text": "In such cases as this, it is, I believe, the established mode to express a sense of obligation for the sentiments avowed, however unequally they may be returned. It is natural that obligation should be felt, and if I could feel gratitude, I would now thank you. But I cannot--I have never desired your good opinion, and you have certainly bestowed it most unwillingly. I am sorry to have occasioned pain to anyone. It has been most unconsciously done, however, and I hope will be of short duration. The feelings which you tell me have long prevented the acknowledgment of your regard can have little difficulty in overcoming it after this explanation."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Mr. Darcy",
      "speak_to": ["Elizabeth"],
      "text": "And this is all the reply which I am to have the honour of expecting! I might, perhaps, wish to be informed why, with so little endeavour at civility, I am thus rejected. But it is of small importance."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Elizabeth",
      "speak_to": ["Mr. Darcy"],
      "text": "I might as well inquire, why, with so evident a design of offending and insulting me, you chose to tell me that you liked me against your will, against your reason, and even against your character? Was not this some excuse for incivility, if I was uncivil? But I have other provocations. You know I have. Had not my own feelings decided against you, had they been indifferent, or had they even been favourable, do you think that any consideration would tempt me to accept the man who has been the means of ruining, perhaps for ever, the happiness of a most beloved sister?"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Elizabeth",
      "speak_to": ["Mr. Darcy"],
      "text": "I have every reason in the world to think ill of you. No motive can excuse the unjust and ungenerous part you acted there. You dare not, you cannot deny that you have been the principal, if not the only means of dividing them from each other, of exposing one to the censure of the world for caprice and instability, the other to its derision for disappointed hopes, and involving them both in misery of the acutest kind."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Elizabeth",
      "speak_to": ["Mr. Darcy"],
      "text": "Can you deny that you have done it?"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Mr. Darcy",
      "speak_to": ["Elizabeth"],
      "text": "I have no wish of denying that I did everything in my power to separate my friend from your sister, or that I rejoice in my success. Towards him I have been kinder than towards myself."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Elizabeth",
      "speak_to": ["Mr. Darcy"],
      "text": "But it is not merely this affair, on which my dislike is founded. Long before it had taken place, my opinion of you was decided. Your character was unfolded in the recital which I received many months ago from Mr. Wickham. On this subject, what can you have to say? In what imaginary act of friendship can you here defend yourself? or under what misrepresentation can you here impose upon others?"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Mr. Darcy",
      "speak_to": ["Elizabeth"],
      "text": "You take an eager interest in that gentleman’s concerns."
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Elizabeth",
      "speak_to": ["Mr. Darcy"],
      "text": "Who that knows what his misfortunes have been can help feeling an interest in him?"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Mr. Darcy",
      "speak_to": ["Elizabeth"],
      "text": "His misfortunes!"
    },
    {
      "speaker": "Mr. Darcy",
      "speak_to": ["Elizabeth"],
      "text": "yes, his misfortunes have been great indeed."
    }
  ]
}

